  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat and humidity persists through mid week with head indices in \nthe upper 90s to near 105F.\n\nLatest analysis shows the offshore subtropical ridge continuing to \nprevail, as a trough from the Great Lakes is moving into the \nNortheast CONUS, leading to zonal flow for the area today and \ntomorrow. At the surface, the Bermuda high remains in place helping \nthe hot and humid conditions before breaking down over the next few \ndays. Temperatures at the time of writing are in the lower 90s with \ndewpoints in the lower 70s. The high temperature will be a few \ndegrees warmer, and with the humidity, heat indices are in the upper \n90s to lower 100s (near 105F in localized areas in Southside hampton \nRoads and into areas of North Carolina between the Chowan River and \nthe Albemarle Sound). \n\nThe hot and humid conditions will continue over the next couple of \ndays. Dewpoints look to mix out some Mon, with heat indices likely \nreaching the upper 90s to lower 100s. The best potential for any \nhead headlines is Tues, as dew points look to be in the lower to mid \n70s with heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s and values \naround 105F in SE VA/NE NC where the cloud cover looks to be less. \nModel ensembles depict a cold front crossing the area late week, \nwhich could help lower temperatures and dew points Fri into the \nweekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Low chance of showers/storms today and Monday with a more \nunsettled pattern returning Tuesday through Thursday. Storms Tuesday \nand Wednesday may bring damaging wind gusts and localized\nflooding.\n\nWith the aforementioned zonal flow across the area today and \ntomorrow, shower/storm chances will be low across the area. Hi-res \nmodels depict isolated pop-up showers across the area today, firing \noff the higher terrain to the west from the lee trough in the \npiedmont or off the bay/sea breeze near the coast. Overall, there is \nonly about a 15-20% chc of showers/storms today. Confidence is low \nin the exact placement or timing, as determining where or when a \nshower may fire up is like guessing which popcorn kernel will pop \nfirst. Coverage looks to be even less Mon with only a stray shower \npossible in the SE Monday aft/evening.\n\nA more unsettled pattern evolves Tues through Thurs. The zonal flow \npattern is expected to break down, as a ridge aloft starts to build \nin Tuesday across the central United States. This will allow for WNW \nflow aloft to return to the area. While at the surface, the Bermuda \nhigh will start to break down slightly and move over the the Gulf \nstates. This could allow for multiple days of active weather (Tues-\nThurs) to return to the area. However, there still remains quite a \nbit of uncertainty at this time, due to some slight disagreement in \nthe models in the lowest levels of the atmosphere. Confidence is \nhighest of shower/storm development on Tues with 30-60% PoPs across \nthe area, highest north. Storm development Wed and Thurs will depend \non leftover boundaries and meso features. SPC has the majority of \nthe area in a Marginal Risk (level 1/5) of severe weather Tuesday \nand a Day 4 15% chance of severe weather, which translates to a \nSlight Risk (level 2/5) for a large portion of the area Wed. \nDamaging winds and localized flooding are the primary concerns with \nany storm.",
